# {{Latn-def|it|name|C|c}}; [[cee]]

=====See also=====
* {{list:Latin script letter names/it}}

====Further reading====
* {{R:it:Treccani|sense=2}}

===Etymology 2===
{{unc|it}}. Rohlfs<ref>Rohlfs, Gerhard. 1969. ''Grammatica storica della lingua italiana e dei suoi dialetti'', vol. 3: ''Sintassi e formazione delle parole''. Torino: Einaudi. §899.</ref> and Von Wartburg<ref>{{R:FEW|4|425|hīc}}</ref> favoured/favored Late Latin {{m|LL.|ecce hīc}}. Maiden<ref>Maiden, Martin. 1995. ''A linguistic history of Italian''. London: Longman. §9.1.1.</ref> casts doubt on this etymology, pointing out that Italian {{m|it|ci}} is an unstressed 'weak' form, while Latin {{m|la|hic}} otherwise survives in Italian only in stressed forms (reinforced by Latin {{m|la|ecce}} or {{m|la|eccum}}) such as {{m|it|ciò}}, {{m|it|qua}}, and {{m|it|qui}}. (It should also be noted that all of the latter trigger syntactic doubling in a following word, thanks to their original final /k/, while {{m|it|ci}} does not.)<ref>{{R:it:DiPI}}</ref> Maiden proposes instead an origin in Latin {{m|la|hince}}, variant of {{m|la|hinc||hence, from here}}, pointing out that in parts of southern Italy there exists a 1PL pronoun {{m|nap|'nci}} (cf. also {{m|nap|nce|'nce}}). Treccani,<ref>{{R:it:Trec|ci1}}</ref> on the other hand, proposes an origin in Latin {{m|la|hīce}}, a variant of {{m|la|hīc||here}}. In any case, the Italian term is certainly cognate with {{cog|nap|ce}}, {{cog|scn|cci}} and {{cog|sdc|zi}}, all three of which share similar adverbial senses, with the latter two also having pronominal senses.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|it|-ci||enclitic}}

====Pronunciation====
{{attention|it|why are there two identical pronunciations?}}
{{it-pr|+,*+<qual:Sicily, Calabria><rhyme:->}}

====Pronoun====
{{head|it|pronoun}}

# [[us]]
#: {{uxi|it|Loro '''ci''' conoscono|They know '''us'''}}
# {{lb|it|reflexive pronoun}} [[ourselves]]; [[each other]]
#: {{uxi|it|'''Ci''' arrabbiamo|We (ourselves) get angry}}
#: {{uxi|it|'''Ci''' amiamo|We love '''each other'''}}
# to [[us]]
#: {{uxi|it|Lui '''ci''' ha detto questo|He said this '''to us'''}}
# {{n-g|replaces the indefinite personal pronoun {{m|it|si||one}} before reflexive {{m|it|si||oneself}}}}; [[one]]
#: {{uxi|it|'''Ci''' si lava.|'''One''' washes oneself.}}
#: {{ux|it|'''Ci''' si annoia quando non c'è niente da fare.|'''One''' gets bored when there is nothing to do.}}
# it, to it
#: {{uxi|it|Non '''ci''' credo.|I do not believe it.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Becomes {{m|it|ce}} when followed by a third person direct object clitic ({{m|it|lo}}, {{m|it|la}}, {{m|it|li}}, {{m|it|le}}, or {{m|it|ne}}).

